export anchor Factory:
    description:
        A tetherable Piton specification for a general-purpose agentic factory
        that delegates work across configurable agents. Each consuming project
        uses this specification to build its own factory. An incoming prompt,
        specification, or both determines what that factory builds.
    projectSpecificRealization:
        This project's deliverable is the factory specification, not a single
        factory implementation. Consuming projects tether the specification
        through Piton and use it to build a factory suited to their project.
        The engineer chooses the implementation language and other appropriate
        implementation choices. Each project's factory grows gradually with
        that project's needs. Keep reusable requirements distinct from those
        project-specific implementation choices. Do not assume one shared
        factory service or centrally shared model-performance history.
    incrementalAdoptionAndEvolution:
        A consuming project initially implements only the factory capabilities
        it needs. As needs grow, its factory can read the tethered factory
        specification and use its own task execution capabilities to dispatch
        work that enhances its implementation. Support this self-evolution
        rather than requiring every specified capability at initial adoption.
        How enhancement needs are identified remains unresolved. Activation
        follows the self-update continuity requirements.
    selfEnhancementAuthority:
        Ask the engineer before initiating factory enhancement tasks by
        default. The engineer may authorize automatic approval of future
        enhancements within the scope of that instruction. Such authorization
        does not itself expand account budgets or other configured limits.
    needDrivenEnhancement:
        Prefer fulfilling an order with existing capabilities when they can
        do the work well. Propose a factory enhancement when a missing
        capability blocks the order or would materially improve delivery,
        especially when it will be reused. Tie self-improvement to project
        needs and follow the established enhancement approval policy.
    selfUpdateContinuity:
        Factory enhancements must preserve ongoing work. The update mechanism
        depends on the technology chosen by the consuming project. Apply
        updates while running when the implementation supports doing so
        without stopping ongoing work. Otherwise wait for a suitable moment
        to restart, or propose a restart to the engineer. Do not mandate one
        universal update mechanism across implementations.
    tetherUpdateReconciliation:
        When an update to the tethered factory specification conflicts with
        project-specific customizations, an agent proposes a reconciliation
        for the engineer to decide. Do not silently give either the updated
        specification or existing customizations precedence.
    orchestration:
        The factory runs independently and owns general orchestration. Agents
        perform explicit execution steps of tasks; no agent owns the general
        orchestration loop.
    integrationPrinciple:
        Deterministic controls with agent-based wiring is a general factory
        design principle. Code implements the controls and their routine
        execution semantics. Agents establish the environment-specific wiring
        for harness connections, build and test commands, remote machines, and
        delivery integrations. Apply this principle beyond remote builds.
        Wiring centers on known factory buttons, meaning defined operations
        with deterministic controls. Those operations must be configurable
        enough for agents to write integration code connecting them to the
        environment. Agents save reusable wiring; ordinary code executes it
        afterward, bringing agents back when wiring needs changes or repair.
        The exact operation set and wiring representation remain open.
    wiringValidation:
        Each factory button defines a standard check that its agent-written
        wiring must pass before use. The check verifies the integration's
        expected behavior, such as running a small remote build and returning
        its result. Exact checks for each operation remain to be defined.
    factoryStateOperations:
        Agents add tasks, revise plans and dependencies, and report results
        through defined factory operations rather than directly editing
        internal factory records or its database. Ordinary code validates
        permissions and consistency and preserves recovery history for these
        changes. Agents may still write project files and integration wiring
        as assigned. The operation transport and storage implementation remain
        choices for the consuming project.
    operationRetries:
        Give factory operation requests identifiers so retries after a lost
        response return the original result without repeating the action.
        Prevent duplicate tasks, builds, or agent launches from retries of the
        same request. The identifier representation and retention mechanism
        remain implementation choices.
    wiringRepair:
        When saved wiring breaks, the factory automatically assigns an agent
        to restore its intended behavior within the existing configuration and
        authorization. Repaired wiring must pass the operation's standard
        check before use. Changes to the intended setup, such as selecting a
        different provider, buying capacity, or changing delivery destinations,
        require engineer input. Failed repairs follow the existing escalation
        path.
    orderSubmission:
        The engineer may discuss work with a chosen conversational agent or ask
        it to break down a feature. When the engineer instructs it to begin, that
        agent can submit an order summarizing its understanding to the factory.
        The conversational agent need not be the agent used for factory planning.
    conflictingOrders:
        When a new order conflicts with active work, delegate assessment of the
        conflict and present a concrete recommendation to the engineer,
        including work that would be replaced or reused. A new order does not
        silently cancel earlier intent. An explicit engineer instruction to
        replace the old approach authorizes proceeding under the existing
        supersession rules. Independent work may continue while clarification
        is pending.
    planningAndExecution:
        A smart, expensive agent plans work at a broad level sufficient for a
        mid-tier agent to elaborate. Mid-tier agents produce specific, detailed
        implementation instructions for cheap, fast implementation agents.
        This hierarchy guides delegation without prescribing a fixed pipeline.
    evolvingWorkflow:
        - An agent proposes the initial steps required to fulfill an order.
        - Steps are deliberately flexible rather than a fixed sequence of stage types.
        - Agents can revise steps as work progresses, within their authority.
        - The factory dispatches execution of the resulting steps.
        - A mid-tier agent proposes the initial workflow by default and can escalate difficult workflow decisions to a smarter agent.
    revisionAuthority:
        Agents can change work below their intelligence tier. Lower-tier agents
        can add steps to request help when required. The receiving agent decides
        whether to answer, escalate to a smarter agent, or disregard the request.
        Disregarding a request means replying to the requesting lower-tier
        agent that it does not need to ask for help with this issue and should
        handle it itself.
        Agents may propose changes to another agent's work at the same
        intelligence tier, but a smarter agent decides those changes. An agent
        may refine its own assigned work within its brief.
    stepContext:
        The delegating agent prepares a focused brief containing the step's
        objective, relevant requirements, specific instructions, expected output,
        and completion criteria. The brief links to the original order,
        applicable Piton specifications, and relevant prior results. The receiving
        agent starts with the brief and can consult broader context when needed
        or request help. The factory preserves briefs and results so handoffs do
        not depend on an agent remembering an earlier conversation.
    dependenciesAndScheduling:
        Agents explicitly declare and revise dependencies between steps. The
        factory enforces those dependencies and determines readiness without
        asking an agent to choose every next step. Independent steps may run
        concurrently once their prerequisites are satisfied, subject to
        configured concurrency limits and available account usage. The factory
        checks for missing prerequisites and circular dependencies before
        executing affected steps.
        Dependency readiness makes a step eligible to run; available capacity
        determines when it starts.
    orderPriorities:
        When orders compete for constrained capacity, favor completing the
        highest-priority order sooner. Other orders may use capacity that the
        higher-priority order cannot use. At equal priorities, favor finishing
        work already underway to avoid accumulating half-finished results.
        Exact priority representation remains a project implementation choice.
    revisionsDuringExecution:
        The design should make revisions affecting running steps rare. When an
        authorized revision does affect running work, the factory marks that
        work as superseded, requests cancellation, and preserves its outputs for
        possible reuse. Replacement steps follow the revised plan. Superseded
        results do not automatically count toward completion.
    concurrentPlanRevisions:
        Treat conflicting concurrent revisions to a plan as a merge conflict.
        The factory dispatches another agent to resolve the conflict, subject
        to the established revision authority. The resolution must preserve a
        coherent plan and its dependencies. The exact conflict representation
        and handling of affected steps while resolution is pending remain
        unspecified.
    unmergedPrerequisites:
        Dependent work may start from a finished prerequisite branch before
        integration once that prerequisite passes its required checks and
        higher-level completion assessment. Track the exact prerequisite
        version used by the dependent work. If the prerequisite changes or is
        rejected, delegate reconciliation before treating the dependent result
        as complete. Merging alone need not block useful dependent work.
    integrationChecks:
        Integration includes checking that concurrent changes work together
        before accepting their merge, even when each change passed separately.
        Combine the changes and run the relevant project-defined checks on
        that combined result. Ordinary code runs the checks; delegate conflicts
        and failures requiring judgment to agents. This requirement does not
        prescribe one universal test suite across projects.
    buildAndTestCapacity:
        The factory manages build and test capacity in addition to agent
        concurrency. Ordinary code queues build and test jobs and enforces
        available capacity; AI agents do not make routine capacity allocation
        decisions. Remote builds are a first-class capability required in the
        initial version. The resource model remains unspecified.
    remoteBuildWiring:
        Remote builds have deterministic controls and behavior implemented in
        code, including queueing, dispatch, and capacity enforcement. Agents
        handle the project- and machine-specific wiring that connects those
        controls to actual remote build environments. Preserve this separation
        between deterministic controls and agent-based wiring. The wiring's
        representation remains unspecified; its reuse, repair, and validation
        follow the general integration principle and wiring validation policy.
    restartRecovery:
        The factory must recover orders after a crash or restart. It preserves
        plans, step status, briefs, results, and worktree references. Before
        resuming, it checks interrupted steps so completed work is not blindly
        repeated. If an agent is still working in its harness after a factory
        restart, reconnect to that session and let it continue rather than
        launching a replacement. Preserve the session references needed for
        this recovery so surviving work is not duplicated.
    failedAttempts:
        The factory allows limited automatic retries for temporary execution
        failures, such as timeouts. Retry limits are configurable. Problems with
        the work itself become help requests through the defined escalation
        path. Temporary execution failures that exhaust automatic retries also
        enter the defined escalation path.
    unlikelyResolution:
        When further autonomous attempts no longer look likely to resolve a
        problem, obtain engineer input rather than continuing unsuccessful
        repair or review cycles. Apply this through the existing escalation
        path and continue independent work where possible. This decision does
        not establish a fixed attempt count or time limit for substantive
        problem resolution.
    spendingLimits:
        API account budgets established during setup are enforced in the
        initial scope. This supersedes the earlier deferral of custom spending
        caps insofar as API account budgets are concerned. Additional per-order
        or factory-wide caps remain on the roadmap. Usage limits also account
        for available spending in an API account and the remaining usage
        allowance on a subscription account.
        Usage and availability should be obtained from the existing harnesses
        or provider APIs as much as possible. When an account runs out of
        available usage, the factory asks the engineer how to proceed.
        Independent work continues where possible under the engineer-input
        policy. When fresh usage readings are unavailable, continue assigning
        work using cautious estimates based on the last known allowance and
        subsequent work. Missing readings alone do not stop dispatch. Estimates
        remain subject to the account limits and budgets; the exact estimation
        method and uncertainty handling remain unresolved.
    resourceOptimization:
        The factory aims for speed, quality, and economy. Initial setup asks
        the engineer how many Claude subscriptions are available, their kinds,
        and the budgets available for API accounts, along with the relevant
        account resources. Maximize use of available subscription allowances
        without exceeding them, and keep API spending within its budgets.
        When appropriate, ask the engineer whether more API funds can be added;
        do not assume that additional funds are authorized.
        Ordinary code continually adjusts resource usage through a PID-style
        feedback algorithm. The target is maximum useful throughput at all
        times within all applicable constraints, without exceeding subscription
        allowances, API budgets, or build capacity. Adapt allocation to the
        current bottleneck. When subscription availability is the constraint,
        maximize effective work within that allowance. When build servers are
        the constraint, maximize useful subscription work in planning at a
        pace aligned with build throughput. Optimize effectiveness rather than
        utilization of an individual resource in isolation.
        The exact measurements, control outputs, tuning, and treatment of
        delayed usage data remain unresolved. A downstream-work priority rule
        has not been adopted as the scheduling policy.
    executionHarnesses:
        The initial supported agent harnesses are Claude Code, Codex, and
        OpenCode. Harness integration must accommodate API-backed accounts and
        subscription accounts. Integration contracts remain unspecified.
        The engineer currently uses Claude, Codex, MiMo, and DeepSeek, with
        OpenCode in the setup. The exact mapping of each model or account to a
        harness has not been confirmed.
    adaptiveModelSelection:
        Model routing depends on thresholds set by the engineer. The factory
        continually researches the changing model landscape and adapts its
        optimization within those thresholds. Evaluate models using returned
        work and higher-tier reviewers' assessments, alongside capability,
        speed, throttling, and API or subscription economics. Model selection
        must evolve with observed results rather than rely on fixed rankings.
        The engineer currently describes DeepSeek Flash as very fast with
        generous throttling, and MiMo as more capable but slower, aggressively
        throttled, and offering a subscription plan. These are the engineer's
        observations, not permanent provider guarantees or verified current
        product claims. The factory asks the engineer before trying a newly
        researched model. Exact thresholds and evaluation methods remain
        unresolved.
    accountAllocation:
        Automatically distribute new work across approved accounts according
        to the best optimization of speed, quality, and economy within the
        engineer's configured thresholds, account allowances, and API budgets.
        Routine allocation among approved accounts does not require approval
        for each switch. Exhausted-account notifications and requests for
        additional funds still follow the engineer-input policies.
    paidFallbackDecision:
        When subscription capacity is temporarily throttled and paid API
        capacity could avoid the wait, ask the engineer to choose between
        spending more to finish sooner and waiting to save money. Available
        API budget alone does not authorize this tradeoff. Routine allocation
        within an already approved policy remains automatic.
    taskSpecificModelEvaluation:
        Learn model strengths and weaknesses separately by kind of work using
        returned results and higher-tier review assessments. Use that history
        to guide routing of similar work. A model's success on routine changes
        must not imply equal suitability for complex numerical algorithms or
        other different kinds of work. Task categories and the exact evaluation
        method remain unresolved.
    outcomeEfficiency:
        Judge model efficiency by the total time and cost to reach an accepted
        result, including review passes and corrections, rather than only the
        first attempt. Smarter models perform a review pass where appropriate
        to assess the result. Use these assessments in the task-specific model
        evaluation, while retaining risk-appropriate review depth.
    engineerInput:
        When the smartest agent determines that a question is for the engineer,
        the factory obtains input from the engineer. While the answer is
        pending, independent work continues as much as possible. Only steps
        that depend on the answer are blocked by that question.
    specificationChangeAuthority:
        When agents discover a gap or contradiction requiring a project
        specification change, they ask the engineer before amending it by
        default. The engineer may grant broad authorization, such as go ahead
        and make all modifications, allowing agents to make specification
        changes within that authorization without seeking approval for each
        change. The scope and duration of that authorization follow the
        engineer's instruction.
    monitoring:
        The engineer can monitor the factory through both a dashboard and
        updates through the conversational agent. The dashboard shows orders,
        steps, agents, and questions requiring engineer input. Update delivery
        mechanisms and frequency remain unspecified.
    engineerControl:
        The engineer initiates work with a prompt and can start or gracefully
        shut down the factory. Routine execution is autonomous; engineer controls
        are deliberately minimal and do not provide per-order pause, resume,
        cancellation, or revision controls for micromanagement. Engineer answers
        to questions escalated by agents remain supported.
    gracefulShutdown:
        On graceful shutdown, the factory stops starting new steps, lets running
        steps finish, and saves pending work for the next start.
    completion:
        Agents and their generated plans and instructions determine completion
        criteria, testing, and reviews, ultimately driven by the prompt,
        specification, or both. The factory does not independently impose
        universal testing or review gates. Desired product outcomes belong to
        individual orders rather than to a fixed category of factory output.
    implementationCompletionHandoff:
        Completion checking is part of the delegated workflow. When an
        implementation agent reports done, a higher-level agent checks what
        that claim means against the assigned work and its expected result.
        Keep this assessment focused; the higher-level agent should not take
        on substantial implementation or investigation itself. Delegate
        further investigation or corrections as needed. This handoff does not
        prescribe a universal test suite or full review pass for every step.
    supportingRoles:
        Different agents also handle merging, testing, and multiple levels of
        review. Completion assessment and integration responsibilities follow
        the defined handoff and integration requirements; other detailed
        responsibilities remain open.
    reviewApproach:
        When a workflow includes review, use a fresh reviewer with a focused
        brief containing the requirements, relevant changes, and checks to run.
        The reviewer forms an initial assessment before consulting the
        implementer's explanation, then may use that explanation to resolve
        specific questions. Match review depth to the work's difficulty and
        risk, reserving full blind review for difficult or high-risk work and
        lighter review for routine changes. This does not impose a universal
        review gate on every order.
    deliveryDestination:
        The order's prompt determines where completed work is delivered, such
        as an integrated branch, a pull request, or the main branch. Asking for
        a delivery default during onboarding is the preferred direction; the
        exact default and fallback behavior remain unresolved.
    workspaceIsolation:
        Git worktrees are the preferred mechanism for isolating concurrent
        implementation work. Allocation and integration policies remain open.
    configuration:
        Initial configuration is developed through a series of prompt questions
        driven by an agent. The resulting configuration is written to an actual
        configuration file. Setup includes the account and budget questions in
        resourceOptimization. Other initial options, file format, and location
        remain unspecified.
    initialOperatingPolicy:
        During onboarding, an agent recommends an initial operating policy
        based on the project's needs, available accounts, and build capacity.
        The engineer reviews and approves that policy. The resource controller
        may tune execution within the approved policy; this does not authorize
        changing its boundaries or increasing budgets without approval.
    illustrativeModels:
        The user's main-agent example is Claude Opus 5.5, mid-tier example is
        Sonnet, and cheap implementation-agent example is DeepSeek Flash. These
        illustrate the intended intelligence and cost hierarchy; they are not
        verified model identifiers or fixed model requirements.
    proposedFoundation:
        DeepSeek Harness is a candidate foundation and is explicitly not
        required. Its official repository is
        https://github.com/deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness. Repository documentation
        describes a plugin-based harness in developer preview. Suitability for
        this factory's orchestration and delegation remains to be evaluated.
    candidateTokenOptimization:
        The engineer is interested in evaluating Jev from TypeSafe AI to reduce
        token usage. Adoption is not decided. Official documentation at
        https://docs.typesafe.ai/introduction/quickstart and the vendor site at
        https://typesafe.ai describe structured decisions with confidence
        estimates. Candidate uses include model-tier routing and context
        relevance scoring; actual savings and decision quality require
        evaluation. These uses are suggestions, not confirmed requirements.
    existingPrototype:
        The engineer already has an ad hoc factory described through a running
        Claude prompt for the Popcorn project at
        /home/hyphyphyph/matter-and-logic/popcorn. Related orchestration artifacts
        live in /home/hyphyphyph/matter-and-logic/popcorn-build.
        Inspected reference documents include popcorn/progress/factory-handoff.md,
        popcorn/factory-dashboard/README.md, popcorn-build/ORCHESTRATOR-HANDOFF.md,
        and popcorn-build/RUNBOOK.md. They document dispatch, planning, merge
        workers, usage monitoring, recovery procedures, a dashboard, and a Jev
        progress service. These are observations about the existing prototype,
        not automatically requirements for the general-purpose factory. The
        running Claude prompt itself has not been inspected.
    initialSuccessCriteria:
        The new factory should be able to resume the engineer's existing
        Popcorn work from its current progress. Popcorn's workflow and operating
        rules must remain project-specific rather than being baked into the
        general-purpose factory. Detailed acceptance scenarios and the exact
        boundary of work to resume remain unresolved.
    existingWorkResumption:
        Support continuing work begun under the existing ad hoc factory rather
        than requiring that work to start over. Represent the project context,
        workflow, and relevant prior progress through the new factory's general
        concepts. The mechanism for carrying over existing artifacts and
        reconciling them with the new factory remains undecided; this does not
        require reproducing the old factory's implementation or fixed pipeline.
        Resumption need not preserve a one-to-one mapping to the old factory.
        Many specifics may be discarded. Inspect the existing factory to inform
        further interview questions about what to include and disregard, rather
        than treating its details as requirements by default.
    evidenceBasedAdoption:
        As a general capability, assess whatever evidence already exists of a
        project's build process, factory, or ad hoc pseudo-factory. Import its
        data and work while transitioning it to the new project-specific
        factory. This applies beyond Popcorn and includes assessment of the
        evidence rather than automatically trusting every existing status or
        requiring all work to start over. The import mechanism and exact
        mapping of existing artifacts remain project-specific decisions.
    openQuestions:
        - Exact contents and representation of orders, tasks, steps, and their handoffs.
        - Scheduling priorities, plan conflict representation, and handling of affected steps during delegated conflict resolution.
        - Build and test resource model.
        - Known factory operation set, integration wiring representation, and per-operation validation checks across harnesses, builds, tests, and delivery.
        - Resolution of peer revision proposals when no smarter tier is available.
        - Configuration of intelligence levels and roles.
        - Engineer-set model-routing thresholds, task categories, and reviewer-based model evaluation methods.
        - Configuration interview contents and configuration file format and location.
        - Detailed responsibilities and handoffs for merging, testing, and review agents.
        - Worktree allocation, integration, and cleanup policies.
        - Recovery reconciliation details and retry defaults.
        - Shutdown handling when running steps hang or await engineer input.
        - Account allowance visibility, cautious usage estimation, and uncertainty handling for unavailable or delayed readings.
        - PID-style resource control measurements, outputs, tuning, and speed-quality-economy tradeoffs.
        - Integration contracts for Claude Code, Codex, and OpenCode.
        - DeepSeek Harness integration and support for the chosen agent providers.
        - Mapping existing Popcorn artifacts and progress into the new factory, the resumption boundary, and detailed acceptance scenarios.
        - Existing Jev progress integration and its relevance to proposed token optimization.
        - Tethered specification updates, identification of self-enhancement needs, and implementation-specific update and restart criteria.
        - Whether and how model-performance learning transfers between project-specific factories.
    specificationStatus:
        Eight interview rounds totaling 65 answered questions are complete.
        The requested final round of 5 questions is complete and incorporated
        into this specification. The interview is finished; remaining open
        questions are recorded explicitly rather than silently assumed.
    interviewFocus:
        No interview question is pending. The final round established engineer
        input when autonomous resolution looks unlikely, need-driven factory
        enhancements, priority-first capacity allocation, general evidence-based
        adoption of existing work, and agent-recommended operating policies for
        engineer approval. Do not automatically begin another interview round
        or implementation work.
